  <h2>Data flow, the way a bench thinks</h2>
  <p>In a FlowLab diagram each node runs once, when every wired input has a value. Two nodes with no wire between them run at the same time. That is how you build a test bench: read a device, parse it, decide whether it is steady, show it, write it down. NI describes LabVIEW as a graphical programming environment for automated production, validation, and research test systems (<a href="https://www.ni.com/en/shop/labview.html" rel="noopener">ni.com</a>), and that is the job FlowLab takes on, from the same side of the table.</p>

  <h2>What is on the bench</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>FlowLab</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Loops and errors</td><td>While and for loops carry a value from one pass to the next. An error travels on a wire of its own.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A front panel</td><td>Controls, indicators, and charts on a panel that opens on a phone.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A debugger</td><td>Highlight execution, probes, breakpoints, and step.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analysis</td><td>Statistics, integral, derivative, and filter nodes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Instruments</td><td>Serial ports and SCPI instruments. Instrument writes are off until someone turns them on, and every write is audited.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data out</td><td>A TDMS log you can open in NI's tools, a node that reads NI hardware, and a durable MQTT output.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>FlowLab has 37 node types of its own beside the ones for messages, and the other tabs in the same flow file keep running exactly as they did.</p>

  <h2>Bring your LabVIEW VIs</h2>
  <p>Import a <code>.vi</code> and FlowLab opens it as a diagram tab with the VI's own wires: the constants with their values, each front panel terminal as a control or an indicator, calls of other VIs by name, loops, and case structures with their bodies. We ran all 425 VIs of NI's open source icon editor through the reader, and the wire count matched the independent reference reader on every one.</p>
  <p>LabVIEW keeps what a primitive does in NI's own table, not in the VI file, so a primitive arrives as a named node wired where it was. Swap it for the FlowLab node that does the same job and the diagram runs. A diagram that guessed would give numbers that look right and are wrong, and on a test bench that is the worst answer there is. <a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/guides/labview-vi-import/">Read the import guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>A bench that is also a plant</h2>
  <p>FlowLab lives inside HotLoop Flow, so the same diagram can read Modbus, OPC UA, EtherNet/IP, S7, and Sparkplug B, and the same deploy records, tracing, and bounded queues cover it. The bench stops being a laptop in a drawer.</p>
